diagnosis_text:
Every layer of the diagnostic sweep was run against it_lab3 and the surrounding fabric and came back clean. At Layer 2, l2_snapshot confirmed all 30 devices have intact, UP interfaces with no duplicate MACs and no missing links — including it_lab3's eth0. At Layer 3, it_lab3 holds the correct address (192.168.1.5/24) with a valid default route to 192.168.1.1, and live pings proved it reaches its gateway with 0% loss (3/3 packets), cross-subnet hosts (office_pc at 192.168.3.2, 0% loss), and internet-side hosts (128.168.0.2, 0% loss). Reciprocal probes from it_lab1 to it_lab3 also succeed. The fabric-wide helpers found no ACL drop rules, no tc shaping, no resource pressure, and no flap or stress processes on any device. The carrier_changes counter on it_lab3's eth0 reads 2, which is the startup-only baseline in Kathara (one down/up transition at container initialisation) and is accompanied by no flap script, no "Network is down" log entries, and no suspicious DHCP/link history. The OSPF daemon is inactive on all three routers, but this is consistent with the statically-routed topology — FRR zebra is running and inter-subnet forwarding works end-to-end. In short, no fault is active or traceable on it_lab3: the workstation has full IP connectivity at this moment, and there is no evidence of a mechanism that could have produced transient isolation. The helpdesk ticket claim is unconfirmed; no corrective action is warranted.
=== END ===